---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { APPS } from '../../data/apps';
import { site } from '../../content';
const title = 'Shopify messaging apps: procurement directory';
const desc = 'Compare channel scope, operating requirements and proposed acceptance checks across current suppliers and historical migration records.';
const path = '/apps';
const apps = [...APPS].sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
const legacy = new Set(['yotpo-email-sms', 'smsbump']);
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <p class="badge">Supplier selection · no numerical ranking</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl leading-8">Start with the job and channel you need, then ask suppliers to demonstrate the relevant workflow. Alphabetical order below is navigation, not an assessment of quality. Catalog exercises are proposed acceptance checks; this directory does not report merchant trials, measured lift or independent product scores.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12 prose-shopify">
    <h2>Choose a procurement path</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Your immediate job</th><th>Start here</th><th>Bring to the evaluation</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Replace a retired sender</td><td><a class="link" href="/apps/yotpo-email-sms">Yotpo migration record</a></td><td>Suppression exports, sending identity and retained Reviews or Loyalty dependencies</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Resolve an acquired account</td><td><a class="link" href="/apps/sendlane">Sendlane continuity review</a></td><td>Renewal terms, active journeys, current product access and a named service owner</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Coordinate browser alerts and text</td><td><a class="link" href="/apps/firepush">Firepush operating review</a></td><td>Independent browser and SMS permissions, purchase exits and alert expiry</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Evaluate a new messaging platform</td><td><a class="link" href="#supplier-table">Supplier scope table</a></td><td>Required markets, channels, event sources, sending volume and staff ownership</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fix a capture bottleneck</td><td><a class="link" href="#acceptance-briefs">Vendor acceptance briefs</a></td><td>Capture promise, permission branching and the destination sender for each channel</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h2>Read channel scope before comparing plans</h2>
    <p>A listed capability is a starting point for an account demonstration. Confirm market eligibility, sender registration, rollout access and plan inclusions before retiring an existing service. Historical names remain here so old links can lead to migration guidance; they are not additional active suppliers.</p>
    <p>Yotpo announced Email and SMS retirement for December 31, 2025; its Reviews and Loyalty products remain separate. Privy announced its Sendlane acquisition in February 2026. Firepush currently advertises SMS and web push. See the linked operating reviews for primary sources and the account-specific questions these changes create.</p>
    <p>Postscript email access, Shopify Messaging channel eligibility and Sequenzy event connections also need explicit confirmation in your proposed setup. Do not assume a dedicated text provider must always be paired with one particular email vendor. Compare a combined platform with separate senders by the actual work required to keep permissions, events and queues consistent.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12" id="supplier-table">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Supplier scope at a glance</h2>
    <div class="card overflow-x-auto mt-6">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>App or historical identity</th><th>Scope</th><th>Commercial question</th><th>Next step</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {apps.map((app) => (
            <tr>
              <td><a class="link" href={`/apps/${app.slug}`}>{app.name}</a>{legacy.has(app.slug) && <p>Historical migration record</p>}</td>
              <td>{app.category}<p>{app.features.join(' · ')}</p></td>
              <td>{app.priceDetail}</td>
              <td><a class="link" href={`#brief-${app.slug}`}>Acceptance brief</a></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12" id="acceptance-briefs">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Take a concrete exercise to each supplier</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-8">These briefs expose the catalog's actual proposed evaluation jobs. They are not quality scores or evidence of a completed test. For legacy messaging records, use the exercise with the destination provider or recovered account data.</p>
    <div class="grid gap-6 mt-8 md:grid-cols-2">
      {apps.map((app) => (
        <article class="card" id={`brief-${app.slug}`}>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-black"><a class="link" href={`/apps/${app.slug}`}>{app.name}</a></h3>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-8">{app.shopifyFit}</p>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-8"><strong>Available capability context:</strong> {app.strengths.join(' · ')}</p>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2">{app.limits.map((limit) => <li>{limit}</li>)}</ul>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
  <article class="container prose-shopify pb-16">
    <h2>Turn a shortlist into a comparable proposal</h2>
    <p>Give every shortlisted supplier the same operating brief: audience by permission and country, average and peak send volume, required event sources, live flows, required support coverage and migration constraints. Ask what is included now, what requires another product, and who will implement each dependency. A feature badge does not assign that work.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Pick one representative journey and write its trigger, audience, delay, purchase exit, opt-out behavior and owner.</li>
      <li>Use test identities to rehearse it, including a missing event, a duplicate event and an excluded recipient.</li>
      <li>Save configuration and queue evidence. Mark each requirement demonstrated, unresolved or dependent on custom work, with a named owner.</li>
      <li>Compare quotes using the same peak-month workload and destination mix. Include service, migration, carrier charges and internal maintenance.</li>
      <li>Approve only the journeys whose acceptance evidence is complete. Release gradually with a documented pause and rollback path.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2>Evaluate total operating cost</h2>
    <p>Keep fixed subscription, usage, message segmentation, pass-through charges, implementation and ongoing service as separate lines. Add the staff time needed to maintain an event bridge or reconcile separate senders. For promotion economics, compare incremental contribution after discounts, returns and communication costs rather than treating platform-attributed revenue as proof of lift.</p>
    <p>Use the <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing worksheet</a> with a current supplier quote. A small current list does not establish future cost: ask how the same workload is billed when contact volume or seasonal traffic increases. Record quote validity and renewal conditions alongside the model.</p>
    <h2>Prevent a technically successful but operationally incomplete migration</h2>
    <p>Assign a single sender to each journey at cutover. Reconcile suppressions before audiences, inspect contacts already waiting in delays, and route replies to an accountable team. Browser permission, email subscription and SMS permission remain separate records; honor a customer's broader request where applicable instead of assuming every opt-out means the same thing.</p>
    <p>For separate platforms, identify how purchase and permission changes cross between them, what happens during an outage and how late events are replayed safely. For a combined platform, still test whether queued messages honor a later purchase or opt-out. Consolidating software does not remove the need to inspect execution.</p>
    <h2>Use the rest of the site with a specific question</h2>
    <p>The <a class="link" href="/compare">comparison directory</a> supplies pairwise routes; use the same acceptance brief for each side. The <a class="link" href="/alternatives">alternatives directory</a> helps frame replacement work. Consult <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination</a> for ownership and the <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent guide</a> for capture records. Verify current supplier terms directly before signing.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
